    title: "Aspirates with ھ, the nasal ں, and vowel marks",
    blurb: "Hear and read the puff of air that ھ adds, the nasal vowel of ں, and the vowel marks that real Urdu leaves out.",
    deck: "Aspirates, nasals and vowel marks",
    body: `
<h3>A puff of air that changes the word</h3>
<p>Hold your hand in front of your mouth and say "pin", then "spin". English p has a puff of air in "pin" and none in "spin", and English speakers never notice. In Urdu that puff makes a different word. <b>پ</b> <i>pe</i> has no puff, like the p of "spin"; add <b>ھ</b> <i>do-chashmī he</i> and you get an aspirated p, with a strong puff: <b>پل</b> <i>pal</i> is a moment, <b>پھل</b> <i>phal</i> is fruit.</p>
{{ex:پل|pal|moment}}
{{ex:پھل|phal|fruit}}
<p>Ten letters take ھ this way. In the romanisation, an h after a letter always means this puff: ph is never f, and th is never the th of "think".</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Aspirated</th><th>Romanisation</th><th>Aspirated</th><th>Romanisation</th></tr><tr><td class="k">بھ</td><td>bh</td><td class="k">ڈھ</td><td>ḍh</td></tr><tr><td class="k">پھ</td><td>ph</td><td class="k">جھ</td><td>jh</td></tr><tr><td class="k">تھ</td><td>th</td><td class="k">چھ</td><td>chh</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ٹھ</td><td>ṭh</td><td class="k">کھ</td><td>kh</td></tr><tr><td class="k">دھ</td><td>dh</td><td class="k">گھ</td><td>gh</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:کھانا|khānā|food; to eat}}
{{ex:ٹھیک ہے۔|ṭhīk hai.|OK. That's fine.}}
{{ex:دودھ|dūdh|milk}}
<p>Compare <b>کہاں</b> <i>kahā̃</i>, "where": the round ہ is a real h after the vowel, not a puff on the k.</p>
<h3>The nasal ں</h3>
<p><b>ں</b> <i>nūn ġunna</i>, nūn ġunna, looks like ن without its dot. It has no n sound of its own: it sends the vowel before it through the nose, as in French "non". The romanisation marks this nasal vowel with a tilde (~).</p>
{{ex:ہاں|hā̃|yes}}
{{ex:میں ٹھیک ہوں۔|maĩ ṭhīk hū̃.|I'm fine.}}
<p>In the middle of a word, the same nasal sound is written with an ordinary ن, as in <b>نہیں</b> <i>nahī̃</i>, "no".</p>
<h3>Vowel marks, and why you will rarely see them</h3>
<p>Urdu can show short vowels with small marks: zabar above for a, zer below for i, pesh above for u. Shadda doubles a letter, and jazm shows that no vowel follows. Children's books and dictionaries use them; real Urdu leaves them out, and so does this course after today. The romanisation fills in what they would show.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Mark</th><th>Example</th><th>Sound</th></tr><tr><td>zabar</td><td class="k">بَ</td><td>ba</td></tr><tr><td>zer</td><td class="k">بِ</td><td>bi</td></tr><tr><td>pesh</td><td class="k">بُ</td><td>bu</td></tr><tr><td>shadda</td><td class="k">بچّہ</td><td>a doubled ch</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:بَ|ba|ba, with zabar}}
<p>Remember the Nastaliq style from Letters 1: signs and books will show these same letters in a sloping hand.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> reading پھ as f, the way English reads "ph". Fruit is <b>پھل</b>, a p with a puff of air: "fal" → <i>phal</i>.</div>
`,
