    id: "hona",
    title: "Pronouns and ہونا: am, is, are",
    blurb: "Say who you are and what someone is: I, you, he, she, we and they with am, is and are.",
    deck: "About me",
    body: `
<p>English has three forms of "be": am, is, are. Urdu has four, all from the verb <b>ہونا</b> <i>honā</i>, "to be". Each pronoun takes its own form, and the form must agree with the pronoun.</p>
<h3>The verb goes last</h3>
<p>English says "I am a doctor". Urdu says "I doctor am": the verb comes at the end of the sentence. Build every sentence in this lesson the same way: first the person, then what they are, then the form of ہونا.</p>
{{ex:میں ڈاکٹر ہوں۔|maĩ ḍākṭar hū̃.|I'm a doctor.}}
<h3>Who takes which form</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Pronoun</th><th>be</th><th>English</th></tr><tr><td class="k">میں</td><td class="k">ہوں</td><td>I am</td></tr><tr><td class="k">تم</td><td class="k">ہو</td><td>you are (to a friend)</td></tr><tr><td class="k">آپ</td><td class="k">ہیں</td><td>you are (polite)</td></tr><tr><td class="k">یہ / وہ</td><td class="k">ہے</td><td>he, she, it, this, that is</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ہم</td><td class="k">ہیں</td><td>we are</td></tr><tr><td class="k">یہ / وہ</td><td class="k">ہیں</td><td>these, those, they are</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:تم خوش ہو۔|tum ḵhush ho.|You're happy. (to a friend)}}
{{ex:آپ مصروف ہیں۔|āp masrūf haĩ.|You're busy. (polite)}}
{{ex:ہم پاکستانی ہیں۔|ham pākistānī haĩ.|We're Pakistani.}}
<p>آپ is plural in grammar even when you speak to one person, so it always takes <b>ہیں</b> <i>haĩ</i>.</p>
<h3>He, she, it: one word</h3>
<p>English chooses between he, she and it. Urdu does not: <b>یہ</b> <i>yeh</i> is someone or something near you, and <b>وہ</b> <i>voh</i> is someone or something farther away. So وہ ڈاکٹر ہے can mean "he is a doctor" or "she is a doctor"; the situation tells you which.</p>
{{ex:یہ کتاب ہے۔|yeh kitāb hai.|This is a book.}}
{{ex:وہ ڈاکٹر ہے۔|voh ḍākṭar hai.|He's a doctor. She's a doctor.}}
<h3>More than one, and respect</h3>
<p>With ہیں, یہ and وہ mean "these" and "they". Urdu also uses this plural for one person you respect, such as a teacher or an older relative: وہ استاد ہیں is "he is a teacher", said with respect.</p>
{{ex:وہ یہاں ہیں۔|voh yahā̃ haĩ.|They're here.}}
{{ex:وہ استاد ہیں۔|voh ustād haĩ.|He's a teacher. (said with respect)}}
<h3>Words you can use now</h3>
<p>Words that never change their shape fit straight into these sentences: <b>ٹھیک</b> <i>ṭhīk</i> fine, <b>خوش</b> <i>ḵhush</i> happy, <b>بیمار</b> <i>bīmār</i> sick, <b>مصروف</b> <i>masrūf</i> busy, <b>پاکستانی</b> <i>pākistānī</i> Pakistani, <b>امریکی</b> <i>amrīkī</i> American, <b>ڈاکٹر</b> <i>ḍākṭar</i> doctor. Words like بڑا change their ending to match the person or thing; that comes in the lesson on adjectives.</p>
<h3>Dropping the pronoun</h3>
<p>Because ہوں only goes with میں, Urdu speakers often leave میں out in a reply: <b>ٹھیک ہوں</b> <i>ṭhīk hū̃</i>, "(I'm) fine".</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using ہے for everything, the way English uses "is" a lot. <b>میں ڈاکٹر ہے</b> → <b>میں ڈاکٹر ہوں</b>, and <b>آپ ڈاکٹر ہے</b> → <b>آپ ڈاکٹر ہیں</b>.</div>
`,
